akr 0.1
project legeos

record legeos.work.acceptance-checks-that-cannot-fail/1 : work {
    title "Nine acceptance and evidence commands name a test filter that matches nothing"
    state proposed
    scope [ path ".akr/records/legeos/**" ]
    intent """
        CORRECTED 2026-09-08, after the first version of this record over-counted. `cargo test`'s
        filter is a SUBSTRING match against the full test path, `crate::module::test_name`, so a filter
        naming a module — `keybindings::tests`, or a bare module name — is live even though that exact
        string appears nowhere in any file's text: the source has `mod keybindings;` in one place and
        `mod tests` in another, and the module name may only ever appear as a FILE NAME. A literal
        content grep calls those phantom and is wrong. The first version of this record listed eleven;
        `consumer_contract` and `migration_0006` are real module files (`git ls-files` matches the
        path, not the content) and `override_dh_auto_configure` is a real target in debian/rules. The
        corrected scan matches a token against tracked file PATHS as well as tracked file CONTENT, and
        excludes .akr/ and docs/generated/ so the ledger cannot vouch for itself.

        THE MECHANISM IS UNCHANGED AND IS THE POINT. `cargo test -p <crate> <filter>` exits 0 when the
        filter matches no test — it prints "running 0 tests ... test result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; N
        filtered out" — so a `command` gate naming a test that no longer exists is satisfied without
        executing anything. It is a gate that cannot fail, and nothing in the ledger can tell it apart
        from one that passed.

        THE CORRECTED FINDING. Of 299 command slots on live records at e6e1b1c3, carrying 104 distinct
        filter tokens, nine match nothing in the tracked source by path or by content:

        - `the_switcher_hides_under_a_window_that_fills_the_screen_and_comes_back_on_a_peek` on
          @legeos.work.the-bottom-edge-belongs-to-the-window-20260908 — FIXED during this pass; the real
          test is `the_switcher_hides_only_under_a_window_that_fills_the_screen`
          (shell/lege-shell/src/surface/app.rs:4765), which covers both halves.
        - `band_buttons_pack_against_the_right_edge_close_last` and
          `a_narrow_band_keeps_close_and_gives_up_the_rest` — @legeos.evidence.hands-on-3-band-controls-20260903
          (verified) and @legeos.work.hands-on-run-3-fixes (active).
        - `the_client_area_sits_under_the_band` and `edges_are_grabbed_just_outside_the_frame` —
          @legeos.evidence.hands-on-3-frame-geometry-20260903 (verified) and
          @legeos.work.hands-on-run-3-fixes (active).
        - `the_field_paints_nothing_in_the_bar_strip` — @legeos.evidence.field-stays-above-the-bar-strip-20260907
          (verified).
        - `wanted_size_is_the_chips_alone_until_a_row_or_controls_need_the_width` —
          @legeos.work.bottom-bar-taskbar (active).
        - `input_settings` and `display_configuration` — @legeos.work.home-field-settings-gui (active).

        STATE CHANGES WHAT EACH ONE MEANS, and it is worth separating them rather than counting to nine.
        None sits on a `completed` record, so nothing in this ledger was completed against a gate that
        executed nothing. Four sit on `active` work, where a filter naming a test that does not exist
        YET is a specification rather than a false gate — home-field-settings-gui has not been built.
        The ones that matter are the four on `verified` evidence recording `result pass`: those are
        assertions in the ledger's own voice about work that was reviewed and closed. They are not
        lying — a command ran and exited 0 — but what they attest to is nothing. The four belong to the
        band and frame-geometry design that the later covers_screen work replaced, which is why the
        honest repair for them is probably to retire the checks rather than rename them.

        WHAT THE NEXT SESSION MUST DECIDE. For each of the four `verified` evidence records: re-run
        against the correct test and revise, or supersede as vacuous. Leaving them is the one option
        that keeps a claim in the ledger that no one can check. For the four on `active` work, nothing
        is needed until completion, but completion must not close them as they stand.

        THE GENERAL FIX, and the constraint on it that is not obvious: a sweep comparing every
        acceptance and evidence command's filter tokens against the tracked source belongs in
        `just check` — but it MUST match the way cargo does, as a substring against the full test path,
        checking file paths as well as file contents. A literal name lookup reproduces the exact
        false-positive class described at the top of this record: on this ledger it would have flagged
        eight healthy gates alongside the nine real ones.
        """
    note """
        RESCAN 2026-10-02 at 50af378 (working tree), against the authoritative list rather than a text grep: `cargo test --workspace -- --list` (2242 tests), each filter token matched as a substring of a test path, as cargo does.

        The four `verified` evidence records the first version worried about are settled; none is rewritten. hands-on-3-band-controls and hands-on-3-frame-geometry no longer appear. bar-cluster-clears-window-controls-20260908 names `the_cluster_never_overlaps_the_maximized_window_controls`, which did exist: `git log -S` shows it added in 57445aa (the evidence was taken from the working tree just before that commit, observed_at 63ab3f7b) and deleted in dbf309d when the bar stopped carrying window controls, so the evidence attested a real run. field-stays-above-the-bar-strip-20260907 names `the_field_paints_nothing_in_the_bar_strip`, which never existed in history, but the same command's other filter ran `a_tile_placed_off_the_grid_is_drawn_back_inside_the_field`, the test its summary describes; the phantom half is noise, not a false pass. home-field-command-checks-20260831, home-field-keyboard-complete (`field_keyboard`) and home-folders-and-panes-20260831 (`desktop_folder`) name tokens that matched source text at their commits; they are historical and left alone.

        LIVE planning checks whose filter matches no test today: bar-cluster-clickable-20260908 (the deleted cluster test; the check is obsolete with the bar redesign), bottom-bar-taskbar (`wanted_size_is_the_chips_alone_until_a_row_or_controls_need_the_width`), hands-on-run-5-fixes (`desktop_folder_opens`), home-field (`field_keyboard`), home-field-settings-gui (`dark_palette_has_every_role_and_light_is_unchanged`, `display_configuration`, `input_settings`; not built yet, so a specification), home-input-and-refinement (`network_control`). None may be completed against those commands as written.

        A LARGER CLASS the first version did not count: 41 live planning records still name pre-rename packages (`-p lege-shell`, `-p lege-comp`, `-p lege-window`, ...). Those commands fail rather than pass vacuously (cargo rejects an unknown package), so they cannot close anything, but every one must be renamed before its record can be completed, and renaming sealed acceptance content puts existing evidence behind the record's last change (see @legeos.papercut.the-lege-templar-rename-never-reached-the). Rename each record when its own work resumes, re-running its checks then, rather than in one sweep that would invalidate ~150 satisfied checks at once.

        The general fix stands: a sweep in `just check` that matches filters the way cargo does. Cheapest correct form found: compare against `cargo test --workspace -- --list` output, which costs one test build (about 45 s warm here).
        """
}

record legeos.work.accessibility-claimed-but-not-built/1 : work {
    title "Either build the AT-SPI bridge and the high-contrast variant, or stop documenting them as shipped"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "distro/seeds/**",
        path "docs/architecture/accessibility.md",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        docs/architecture/accessibility.md states that AT-SPI and Orca ship, and that every shell surface has a high-contrast variant. Neither is true in code. No Cargo.toml in the workspace depends on atspi; no accessible-object tree is registered and no a11y bus is spoken anywhere in shell/lege-shell or crates/. No high-contrast token, theme or setting exists in the shell. distro/seeds/a11y.seed really does install orca, at-spi2-core and speech-dispatcher — which is what makes this worse than a plain omission: a blind user can install LegeOS, start Orca, and meet a desktop that says nothing, because the shell paints itself with a custom rasterizer and exposes no name, role or state for any tile, drawer row or widget region. A toolkit would have carried the bridge; painting everything means owning it.

        What is real: the focus ring is a genuine distinct indicator, reduced motion is a respected setting, and the field is deliberately keyboard-complete with pointer parity enforced by a gate. Those are the foundations; they are not a screen reader.

        Two defensible outcomes and one indefensible one. Build the bridge; or correct the documentation and the seed to say plainly that assistive-technology support is not yet implemented. What must not persist is the current state, where the docs assert a capability a user could depend on and be stranded by. Correcting the documentation costs almost nothing and should not wait for the implementation — that part is separable and worth doing first, whatever is decided about the rest.
        """
    note """
        Ranked fifth for daily-work cost, but the false documentation is a defect independent of that ranking and is cheap to fix on its own.
        """
    derived_from [ @legeos.assessment.home-field-usability-gaps-20260831 ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.m2-session-runs ]
}

