record legeos.work.home-widgets/1 : work {
    title "Home Field widgets — every missing desktop capability as a tile on the one screen"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "cli/templar/**",
        path "crates/templar-appd/**",
        path "crates/templar-types/**",
        path "interfaces/dbus/**",
        path "services/templar-sessiond/**",
        path "shell/templar-shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        A gap review of the Home Field against what any desktop of the last twenty years offers found that a cold session gives the user eight drawer tiles and the message "open an application and it will appear", with no way to open one. Application tiles are derived only from windows that are already running and are removed when the last one closes, so the launcher route is circular. There is no logout, restart, shutdown, suspend or lock; no way to close a window; no volume, brightness or battery; no notification rendering despite sessiond hosting the notification server; no context menus; and the field's own search bar does nothing outside an expanded drawer. The decision is that each of these arrives as a widget on the single screen — information first, sub-clickable, opening a window only as a deliberate second step — rather than as a panel, a dock or a tray, which is consistent with M2's taskbar-free commitment and with homefield.md's rule that the launcher is inventory and the home screen is working memory. @legeos.work.home-widget-foundations built the framework; this item is the umbrella for the widgets themselves and for the window controls that are still missing.
        """
    note """
        Each child is independently landable against the finished foundation. Read @legeos.work.home-widget-foundations' note first: it records the constraints the user set (clamp rather than evict, navigation-only contiguity, session power over logind) which apply to every child here.

        Two rules from the foundation govern all of this work. The shell renders and forwards; policy lives in a service behind a D-Bus interface, so each widget needs a backing interface before it needs pixels — Lifecycle1 is the worked example to copy. And every capability must be declared once in lege_types::action, which is what puts it in the widget's regions, the cheat sheet and `lege` together; `cargo run -p xtask -- check-actions` fails the build when a surface performs an action nobody declared.

        Amended 2026-09-08 (@legeos.work.home-purity-20260908). The widget contract stands and so does the capability review that produced this umbrella: every gap named in the intent is still a gap that must be filled, and the shell-renders/service-decides and declare-once-in-the-manifest rules are unchanged. What changes is *where* three of them are shown. Session, Notifications and Settings are the machine's business, and docs/garden/garden.md puts the machine on the bar and the user's own things on Home, so those three leave Home for the bar's cluster (@legeos.work.bottom-bar-taskbar) and Home places no `TileKind::System` tile at all. Notifications keep no surface of their own on Home: a new arrival is the transient `Layer::Notification` toast `surface/app.rs::ensure_toast` already creates, and the history is behind the bar's cluster. The three `WidgetDescriptor`s remain declared but inert — nothing places them — and are the bar cluster's contract to draw against.
        """
    depends_on [
        @legeos.constraint.services-not-indicators,
        @legeos.work.home-widget-foundations
    ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.m2-session-runs ]
}

record legeos.work.home-work-centred-folder-clusters-20261004/1 : work {
    title "Home's active work leaves the bottom row: window cards sit on free field cells around the centre, clustered by folder (garden link, else recent history), and never move when parked"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "docs/architecture/homegarden.md",
        path "docs/garden/garden.md",
        path "shell/README.md",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/drawers/**",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/surface/offscreen.rs",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/work_layout.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        User request (2026-10-04, from Lubuntu): the active-window cards on Homegarden (terminals, folders and images together; click to open; a minimized window minimizes to its card) must not be a dedicated bottom row, but placed by a logic that starts them around the centre of the screen and organizes them by folder location when more than one is active, or from recent history. Built: the paginated bottom rail (work_regions, paging, PageUp/PageDown, header) is gone and the persistent grid gets the whole area. shell/src/work_layout.rs (pure) places each window's card on grid cells free of persistent tiles (3x2, 2x1 only when nothing larger fits): windows sharing a folder form a cluster, unfiled terminal/folder/image windows another, everything else a third; clusters ordered by their most recent member start at the grid centre, members gather around their cluster's centroid, other clusters keep a one-cell gap where possible. Existing slots are kept through activation, parking and restoring, so a parked window's card stays where it was; only grid or zoom changes re-arrange everything, and a persistent tile dropped on a card moves only that card. A window's folder is its garden Place association, else the folder its application last touched in Files.Recents1 (DrawerFeed.folders: the entry for a directory mime, else its parent); titles are never read and place stays garden-only. WorkKind::Image covers Graphics apps with Viewer/Photography/RasterGraphics. Windows that fit nowhere are counted on one '+N windows / Show all' card that opens the window overview (window.show-all).
        """
    note """
        Uncommitted on developer-edition-0-1 over 2bc8f74 with the user's WIP. Revises the layout part of the completed @legeos.work.homegarden-active-work-calendar-20261003 (its contiguous paginated region). Known limits: the overflow card is reachable by pointer, not Tab; on a small output with a full field every window may collapse into the overflow card; no shrink-to-card animation exists — 'minimizes to that area' is the card staying in place, the compositor knows nothing of card rects. Persistent tiles grow taller once because the rail no longer reserves height.
        """
    acceptance {
        check headless-smoke {
            statement """
                templar-shell --render-home-work exits 0 and its wide render shows the cards gathered around the centre in folder clusters with no bottom row.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.home-work-centred-clusters-render-20261004 ]
        }
        check session-cards-centre-and-cluster {
            statement """
                In the installed session with a terminal and a file-manager window in one repository, an image viewer on a photo folder and a browser: revealing Home shows their cards on free space around the centre, the repository's windows next to each other, the browser apart; no bottom work row; saved tiles untouched.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check session-crowded-field {
            statement """
                In the installed session with more windows than free cells: a '+N windows / Show all' card appears and opens the window overview; dragging a saved tile onto a card moves only that card.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check session-park-stays-put {
            statement """
                In the installed session: parking a window (Park chip, the window's minimize, or the bar) and revealing Home shows its card in exactly the place it was, marked parked; clicking it restores that window; activating other windows moves no card.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check session-recent-folder {
            statement """
                In the installed session: a window whose application has no garden association but recently opened a file (e.g. an image viewer opening a photo) is placed next to other work in that file's folder, its label reading 'near <folder>', while its menu offers no Place actions for that folder.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check shell-tests {
            statement """
                templar-shell tests and clippy pass, including work_layout's placement, stability, overflow and grid-change tests and the recent-folder parsing test.
                """
            method command
            command "CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/legeos-clippy-target cargo test -q -p templar-shell"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.home-work-centred-clusters-tests-20261004 ]
        }
    }
    derived_from [ @legeos.work.homegarden-active-work-calendar-20261003 ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.developer-edition-0-1 ]
    verified_by [
        @legeos.evidence.home-work-centred-clusters-render-20261004,
        @legeos.evidence.home-work-centred-clusters-tests-20261004
    ]
    author "dk"
}

