record legeos.work.native-pixelkit-filechooser-cutover-20261007/1 : work {
    title "Native PixelKit FileChooser replaces LXQt portal; PCManFM and policykit retained"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "COMPOSITOR.md",
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/{xdg_shell,state,xwayland}.rs",
        path "crates/templar-window/src/service.rs",
        path "debian/control",
        path "distro/image/**",
        path "distro/metapackages/**",
        path "distro/seeds/**",
        path "portals/**",
        path "session/templar-session/src/main.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Owner request 2026-10-07: replace only the LXQt FileChooser portal with the existing Templar-native PixelKit picker. Preserve PCManFM-Qt as the file manager and lxqt-policykit-agent as the authentication agent. Make Templar the sole vendor FileChooser selection, remove the LXQt portal from package/seed dependencies, preserve higher-priority user portal overrides, retain the freedesktop frontend/document portal and GTK for its unrelated interfaces. Repair concrete native chooser consumer failures discovered while proving the cutover. No live desktop deployment/restart/logout by the agent.
        """
    note """
        This explicit owner direction replaces the earlier S01/S02/default-picker documentation choice to keep FileChooser=lxqt;gtk. It does not claim physical or installed-session release acceptance, and does not change the independent Background/capture policy or broader S00-S19 gates.
        """
    acceptance {
        check browser-wayland {
            statement """
                Actual Firefox and Chromium on an owned nested Wayland compositor select and read known local file bytes through the genuine frontend/document portal at least three times each, cancel and complete Save As with byte verification. Native dialog parenting is observed with actual parented browser requests.
                """
            method observation
        }
        check native-contract {
            statement """
                Selected save filter reaches the portal reply; replacing an existing target requires deliberate confirmation; cancellation, simultaneous dialogs and backend restart retain honest request outcomes. Focused portal regressions pass.
                """
            method observation
        }
        check native-routing {
            statement """
                Vendor FileChooser selects Templar only, portal package and generated session/image composition do not require xdg-desktop-portal-lxqt, PCManFM-Qt and lxqt-policykit remain selected. User overrides remain untouched.
                """
            method observation
        }
        check package-delivery {
            statement """
                Updated portal package is built and inspected without installation; package generation/closure and packaging gates run; existing portal documentation describes the native default and records evidence plus unexercised physical limits.
                """
            method observation
        }
    }
    part_of [ @legeos.work.developer-edition-daily-driver ]
    author "dk"
}

record legeos.work.no-memory-ceiling-on-developer-tooling/1 : work {
    title "Nothing on this machine stops one process taking all of it, and the session survives just enough to be useless"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "compositor/templar-comp/src/**",
        path "distro/**",
        path "services/templar-hostd/**",
        path "session/units/**",
        path "shell/templar-shell/src/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Close the gap @legeos.observation.one-developer-tool-froze-the-machine-for-45-minutes-20260908
        measured: a single rust-analyzer at 19.9 GB RSS exhausted 31 GB of memory and 7.8 GiB of swap,
        the load average reached 57.89, no OOM killer fired, no cgroup constrained anything, and the
        compositor and shell both stayed alive and unresponsive for 45 minutes. Killing one process
        fixed it instantly. LegeOS is meant to be a machine a developer can work on and build the
        system itself on, so a build tool being able to do this to it is a distro-level defect and not
        a user error.

        NOT DESIGNED HERE. This record exists so the incident is not lost and so the choice is made
        deliberately by the user rather than by whoever hits it next. Four levers are on the table and
        they are not alternatives so much as layers:

        (1) A memory ceiling on developer tooling. systemd slices already exist and LegeOS already
        writes unit files: a dev-tools.slice with MemoryMax and MemoryHigh would have capped the
        indexer at a number the user chose, and the tool would have been reclaimed or killed instead of
        the machine. Cheapest and most targeted, and it fits the existing session/units layout.

        (2) A userspace pressure daemon. The kernel OOM killer did not fire, which is the normal and
        much-documented behaviour when a machine is thrashing rather than allocating: it is making
        progress, very slowly, so nothing declares an emergency. earlyoom, systemd-oomd or nohang act
        on PSI pressure instead of on allocation failure, which is exactly the signal this incident
        produced.

        (3) zram in place of, or in front of, a swap partition. Seven point eight gigabytes of disk
        swap is what turned an out-of-memory condition into forty-five minutes of page faults. A
        compressed in-memory swap fails faster and more honestly, which on a laptop is the better
        failure.

        (4) A route out from inside the session. The compositor stayed alive, which is the good news
        and the bad news: the user could see their desktop and could do nothing with it. Whatever else
        is decided, lege-comp should reserve a way to reach a process list and kill something under
        memory pressure — a compositor-owned chord that does not depend on the shell, the way the lock
        screen is compositor-owned rather than client-owned.

        WHAT THE NEXT SESSION MUST DECIDE, in this order: whether (1) alone is enough for the reference
        machine, or whether (2) is wanted as the general answer for a distro that will run on hardware
        with less memory than this one; whether (3) changes the installer's storage plan, since
        @legeos.decision.independent-storage-roles and the provisioner currently lay out a swap
        partition; and whether (4) is in scope for Developer Edition 0.1 or belongs after it. Only (4)
        touches the compositor; (1) and (3) touch the installer and the unit files; (2) is a package
        selection and a policy.

        One measurement worth taking before deciding anything: what the same indexing run costs under
        a MemoryMax that is generous but finite. If it completes, (1) is the whole answer for this
        machine and the rest is for other hardware.
        """
}

record legeos.work.ownership-tier-declaration-20261002/1 : work {
    title "The ownership tiers as data the build checks: every subvolume, state path and component names its tier and owner, and every crossing is a declared exception"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "debian/**",
        path "distro/deploy/README.md",
        path "distro/deploy/components.toml",
        path "distro/deploy/layout.toml",
        path "docs/architecture/**",
        path "justfile",
        path "session/units/**",
        path "tools/xtask/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Make @legeos.decision.three-ownership-tiers checkable before anything is changed to obey it, the same way the service inventory became data in distro/deploy/components.toml (@legeos.work.event-driven-service-audit).
        - distro/deploy/layout.toml: each subvolume in [root] (@, @home, @var, @snapshots, @deployments) and the [data] role gains tier = system | state | work and owner = <component or 'person'>. State paths that matter get the same: /var/lib/templaros/hostd, /var/lib/templaros/snapshots, /var/lib/dpkg, $XDG_STATE_HOME/templaros.
        - distro/deploy/components.toml: each component gains tier and writes = [declared paths]; an [[ownership.exception]] table names each crossing with the decision that allows it (hostd snapshots @home for index checkpoints; templar dev deploy runs apt on the live root; the provisioner's chroot apt; scripts/update-templaros.sh's apt advice), so the list of leaks is in one file and shrinks visibly.
        - xtask check-ownership, wired into just check: fails when a subvolume, state path or component has no tier; when a package's debian/*.install puts a file under /home, /var/lib/templaros of another component, or anywhere a Work-tier path; when a unit (session/units/**) has StateDirectory/ReadWritePaths/ProtectHome settings that contradict its component's tier and writes; when an exception names no decision.
        - docs/architecture/ownership-tiers.md: the three tiers, what Ubuntu owns and what Templar owns, where apt sits, the exception list read from components.toml (pointer, not a copy).
        """
    note """
        Reports today's leaks as exceptions rather than fixing them; steps 2-4 of @legeos.work.ownership-tiers-20261002 remove them. The tests are written against fixture trees so they fail without the check, not against the live repository's current shape alone.
        """
    acceptance {
        check check-catches-crossings {
            statement """
                Fixture tests fail the check for: a component without a tier, a deb installing under /home, a unit with ReadWritePaths into another component's state, an exception with no decision; and pass the corrected fixture.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p xtask ownership"
        }
        check in-the-gate {
            statement """
                just check runs check-ownership.
                """
            method command
            command "just check"
        }
        check tree-declares-every-tier {
            statement """
                On the repository, every subvolume, listed state path and component declares a tier and owner, every known leak is an exception naming its decision, and the check passes.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run -q -p xtask -- check-ownership"
        }
    }
    implements [ @legeos.decision.three-ownership-tiers ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.ownership-tiers-20261002 ]
}

record legeos.work.ownership-tiers-20261002/1 : work {
    title "Begin the three ownership tiers: declare them as checked data, put hostd's deployments where the layout says, make the system read-only behind one way in, and give terminal workloads their own identity"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "cli/templar/**",
        path "crates/templar-appd/**",
        path "crates/templar-types/**",
        path "distro/**",
        path "docs/architecture/**",
        path "installer/**",
        path "services/templar-hostd/**",
        path "services/templar-resourced/**",
        path "session/**",
        path "tools/xtask/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Carry @legeos.decision.three-ownership-tiers from statement to enforcement in four steps, ordered so each is useful alone:
        1. @legeos.work.ownership-tier-declaration-20261002 — the tiers as data the build checks (every subvolume, state path and component names its tier and owner; crossings are declared exceptions) and one architecture document. Cheap, and it turns every later leak into a failing check instead of a review comment.
        2. @legeos.work.hostd-deployments-follow-the-layout-20261002 — a correctness fix independent of the decision's open choices: candidates live at @deployments/<id> and boot entries name that subvolume.
        3. @legeos.work.read-only-system-deployment-20261002 — the booted deployment read-only, the package database moving with it, and apt reachable only through a hostd transaction; dev deploy and the update script converted or declared. Waits for the owner's answers (a) and (b).
        4. @legeos.work.terminal-workload-identity-20261002 — work started from a terminal gets its own unit and an identity naming its parent application; resourced sees workloads and Flatpak scopes. Waits for answer (c).
        Not begun here: the application record as the full authority (permissions, sandbox policy acted on, update source) beyond what step 4 needs, and moving further applications to Flatpak (answer (d)).
        """
    note """
        Everything here is exercised with cargo tests, the provisioner's disk-image path (tests/integration/provision-qemu.sh) and nested sessions; nothing touches the owner's live session or the installed root. Related, not merged: @legeos.work.self-hosting-legeos and @legeos.work.full-distribution-roadmap.
        """
    acceptance {
        check no-undeclared-crossing {
            statement """
                The tier check from step 1 passes on the tree with no undeclared crossing, and its exception list contains only what the decision names.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run -q -p xtask -- check-ownership"
        }
        check steps-complete {
            statement """
                The four step records are completed against their own checks, or carried into a revised plan with a disposition each.
                """
            method observation
        }
    }
    implements [ @legeos.decision.three-ownership-tiers ]
    author "dk"
    source {
        kind external
        role origin
        document "nitrux-ownership-tiers-advice-20261001"
        use """
            The request to formalize and enforce the three ownership layers.
            """
    }
}

record legeos.work.partition-manager-in-the-installer-20260909/1 : work {
    title "A partition manager on the live medium, and an installer that will not be pointed at the wrong partition"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "distro/seeds/selection.toml",
        path "installer/CHECKLIST.md",
        path "installer/provision.sh",
        path "installer/templaros-install",
        path "tests/integration/provision-qemu.sh"
    ]
    intent """
        The provisioner already refuses to be careless — --free-space creates only the two partitions it names and touches no existing one, --esp/--root formats two partitions and never writes the table, every mode prints a plan and changes nothing without --apply — but it says all of that in a terminal, in the language of device nodes. Nothing shows the owner what is on the disk before they choose, and there is no way to make the free run in the first place from inside the medium.

        Two parts, both reusing what exists rather than writing a partitioner:

        1. gparted joins the live layer in distro/seeds/selection.toml (live medium only; the installed system gets none of it), and `legeos-install --partition` launches it, waits, then re-reads the table with partprobe and re-plans. GParted is the partition editor a person may already know, it is the one that will not let a mounted filesystem be moved, and writing another one is the mistake this is meant to prevent.

        2. `legeos-install` grows a disk step it prints before anything else: every partition on the target device with its size, type, filesystem, label and — if it is mounted or holds a mounted filesystem — where; the largest free run; and which two partitions the plan would use, named. It refuses, rather than warns, on a partition that is mounted, that is the ESP the live medium itself booted from, or that holds the running system.

        The hazard this exists for is concrete on the reference laptop: /dev/nvme1n1p4 is the intended LegeOS root and is at this moment mounted as the ISO build volume, holding the archive and a 4 GB ISO. An installer that formats what it was pointed at would take the medium being installed from with it.
        """
    note """
        Deliberately not Calamares: it is an installer, not a partition manager, and adopting it would replace the declarative provisioner, its plan/apply split and its build-set archive with a different model. GParted is the existing thing the ladder asks for — one package on the live medium and one flag.
        """
    acceptance {
        check gparted-on-the-medium {
            statement """
                The live seed carries gparted and the installed system does not; `legeos-install --partition` launches it in the live session and re-reads the table when it exits.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run -q -p xtask -- check-packaging"
        }
        check refuses-a-mounted-target {
            statement """
                --esp/--root naming a mounted partition, the live medium's own ESP, or a partition holding the running system exits non-zero and writes nothing, with --apply given.
                """
            method command
            command "tests/integration/provision-qemu.sh"
        }
        check the-plan-shows-the-disk {
            statement """
                A dry run against the QEMU fixture prints every existing partition with size, type, filesystem and mount state, names the free run, and names the two partitions it would use.
                """
            method command
            command "tests/integration/provision-qemu.sh"
        }
    }
    after [ @legeos.work.iso-refresh-at-current-head-20260909 ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.developer-edition-0-1 ]
}

record legeos.work.pcmanfm-archive-menu-20261003/1 : work {
    title "PCManFM multi-selection ZIP compression and safe broad-format extraction"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "Cargo.lock",
        path "Cargo.toml",
        path "cli/README.md",
        path "cli/templar/**",
        path "crates/templar-archive/**",
        path "debian/**",
        path "docs/architecture/files.md",
        path "scripts/build-in-resolute.sh"
    ]
    intent """
        Add PCManFM-Qt archive custom actions shipped in templaros-cli: immediately visible
        Compress to ZIP and an Extract submenu containing Extract here, Extract to smart new
        folder here, and Extract to other directory with a popup directory chooser. One ZIP
        holds every selected source; extraction handles every selected archive, including mixed
        formats, with bounded streaming buffers, cancellation and visible errors. Preserve source
        files, reject unsafe archive paths/links, and never silently overwrite output files.
        """
    acceptance {
        check archive-behavior {
            statement """
                Multi-selection ZIP creation and common archive/compression extraction execute with exact payloads, smart naming, collision protection, unsafe-path refusal and cancellation cleanup.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.pcmanfm-archives-behavior-20261003/1 ]
        }
        check delivery {
            statement """
                Both packaging paths carry the custom actions and declare the required archive runtime and dialog dependency; native archive and CLI builds complete.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo build -p templar && cargo test -p templar-archive && cargo xtask check-packaging"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.pcmanfm-archives-delivery-20261003/1 ]
        }
        check menu-and-popup {
            statement """
                The actual file manager shows Compress to ZIP directly and the three Extract submenu actions; selecting another directory opens a popup and cancel performs no extraction.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.pcmanfm-archives-menu-20261003/1 ]
        }
    }
    part_of [ @legeos.work.pcmanfm-qt-integration ]
    author "dk"
}

record legeos.work.pcmanfm-qt-integration/1 : work {
    title "pcmanfm-qt as the file manager: the role, the composition, and the three routes into filed"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "cli/lege/**",
        path "crates/lege-session-core/src/providers.rs",
        path "debian/legeos-cli.install",
        path "distro/app-manifests/**",
        path "distro/deploy/components.toml",
        path "distro/seeds/selection.toml",
        path "docs/architecture/files.md",
        path "services/lege-filed/src/bookmarks.rs",
        path "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/app.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Make `pcmanfm-qt` the session's file manager and take `lege-files` out of the composition. `ProviderRole::FileManager` becomes a derived capability off `Categories=FileManager` (`lege-session-core/src/providers.rs`), pcmanfm-qt at priority 100; `distro/app-manifests/org.legeos.Files.json` is deleted, so nothing claims the role or `inode/directory` on Files' behalf and no shipped manifest names an uninstalled executable. `FieldAction::OpenInFiles` stops hardcoding `org.legeos.Files` and resolves the role through the providers map (`app.rs::open_in_file_manager`), reusing the "Settings ▸ Providers" sentence every unfilled role uses. Seeds swap `lege-files` for `pcmanfm-qt` plus `qt6-wayland`; the `files` component stays in `distro/deploy/components.toml` because that matrix is the build set, not the image. Integration: two DES-EMA custom actions shipped by `legeos-cli` over two new CLI subcommands (`lege home place`, `lege project create-here`), and a one-way bookmarks export from filed to `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gtk-3.0/bookmarks`.
        """
    note """
        Two things the packet asked for turned out not to need doing. `session/lege-session` needs no `QT_QPA_PLATFORM`: pcmanfm-qt was launched against the nested compositor with the variable unset and Qt selected the Wayland plugin itself (`qt.qpa.wayland` in its own log; `lege_comp::xdg_shell toplevel mapped window_id=WindowId(5) layer=Some(Normal)` in the compositor's). And filed's `Trash1` already follows the freedesktop Trash specification on exactly the directories libfm-qt uses, so Home's Trash and pcmanfm-qt's are the same trash with no code written.

        "Search here" is deferred rather than shipped: FSearch has no option to scope a search to a folder (`app.rs::search_everywhere` already says so), and pcmanfm-qt has its own Find. A menu entry that silently searched everywhere would be a lie.

        `legeos.work.standalone-file-manager` is `completed`, not live, so there was nothing to supersede; this record cites it through the decision's `derived_from` instead.
        """
    acceptance {
        check composition {
            statement """
                The seeds and the legeos-desktop metapackage name pcmanfm-qt and qt6-wayland and no longer name lege-files, and every generated file matches the selection.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run -q -p xtask -- seeds --check && cargo run -q -p xtask -- check-packaging"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check filed-routes {
            statement """
                The two CLI subcommands the custom actions call exist and are tested, and a bookmark mutation rewrites the GTK bookmarks file libfm-qt reads.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege create_here && cargo test -p lege-filed bookmarks"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check hands-on-confirmation {
            statement """
                At the desktop: opening a folder from Home opens a pcmanfm-qt window with LegeOS chrome; "Add to Home" in its context menu puts that folder on the Home Field as a tile; "Create project here" makes a project named for the folder; a folder bookmarked in Home appears in pcmanfm-qt's Places.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check role-and-launch {
            statement """
                The FileManager role is derived from Categories=FileManager with pcmanfm-qt at priority 100, and nothing in the shell names an application to open a folder.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-session-core providers && cargo test -p lege-shell"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.bar-ground-and-pcmanfm-role-20260907/1 ]
        }
        check runs-under-lege-comp {
            statement """
                pcmanfm-qt opens a folder window on lege-comp with LegeOS chrome and no QT_QPA_PLATFORM in its environment.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.pcmanfm-qt-under-lege-comp-20260907/1 ]
        }
    }
    implements [ @legeos.decision.pcmanfm-qt-is-the-file-manager ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.developer-edition-0-1 ]
}

