record legeos.work.wgsl-effects-library-essence-transition-20261007/1 : work {
    title "A WGSL effect library: the essence transition (windows flowing through the gaps to their Home card or the Homegarden) and surface materials, tuned in a standalone sandbox"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "ARCHITECTURE.md",
        path "Cargo.toml",
        path "crates/templar-effects/**",
        path "tools/README.md",
        path "tools/effects-sandbox/**"
    ]
    intent """
        User request (2026-10-07): a WGSL effect library in LegeOS, starting with an advanced fade for the desktop button, minimize and restore — a window deconstructs to essence and flows toward the Homegarden along the paths of least resistance, through the gaps around the windows that stay and never visibly through them; parts over other windows vanish fast (about 50 ms), parts touching the garden linger so the rest of the essence can reach that gap; the reverse brings a window back; 50-300 ms; cheap enough to happen quickly and repeatedly. Then surface materials for sparing use (notifications, the login screen and its transition, Home's ground): glass, etched glass, stained glass, stone, glassy stone, metal sheen, rough wood. Tested in a standalone sandbox from window rectangles alone, not in a session. Implemented as crates/templar-effects (no dependencies: WGSL sources, the essence planner, noise tile, material uniforms) and tools/effects-sandbox (its own Cargo workspace with wgpu/winit/naga, like helpers/templar-preview-codecs). Planning follows the transferable results of the Bend staged-effects experiments: plan once per trigger on a bounded field, retain, skip idle frames, reuse a small noise lattice rather than a full-resolution basis.
        """
    note """
        Desktop button sends each window into its own Home card by default (H toggles to soaking into the nearest gap): with nothing beneath a window, soaking is a plain in-place dissolve. Not committed (another agent shares the tree). Compositor integration is not started: Smithay custom shaders are GLSL ES 1.00 and naga emits ES 3.00 at minimum; the route is GlesFrame::with_context with the ES 3.00 output after verifying GL_VERSION on both GPUs, or an ES 1.00 port. deps-check purity rule for templar-effects deferred because tools/xtask/src/main.rs carries another agent's uncommitted edits.
        """
    acceptance {
        check automated {
            statement """
                templar-effects tests and pedantic clippy pass: covered pieces vanish within the covered time, garden gaps hold until their essence arrives, paths cross a wall only through its gap, card paths land where each piece sits in the card, planning is deterministic and allocation-reusing, the field is bounded at any resolution, a screen with no garden dissolves in place, runs reverse without a jump.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -q -p templar-effects && cargo clippy -q -p templar-effects --all-targets -- -D warnings"
        }
        check bounded-cost {
            statement """
                On the reference laptop a maximized-window plan takes about 1-3 ms at 1080p-4K and the per-frame GPU cost stays a fraction of a frame on both the RTX 4060 and the Iris Xe.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run --release --manifest-path tools/effects-sandbox/Cargo.toml -- --bench [--size 3840x2160]"
        }
        check compositor-integration {
            statement """
                In a nested session the compositor draws park/unpark and the desktop button with the essence transition in place of motion.rs, from WindowService::stacking_order and the minimize targets, honouring reduced motion.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check owner-visual-review {
            statement """
                The owner judges the essence transition (minimize, restore, desktop button into cards and soaking, mid-flight reversal, 50-300 ms) and the seven materials acceptable in the interactive sandbox, or names what to change.
                """
            method manual
        }
        check shader-gate {
            statement """
                Every WGSL entry point parses and validates under naga 30 and translates to GLSL ES 3.00 and SPIR-V.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo run --release --manifest-path tools/effects-sandbox/Cargo.toml -- --check"
        }
    }
    author "claude"
}

record legeos.work.window-controls-and-mouse-parity/1 : work {
    title "Window close and resize, and a pointer path for every action that today has only a chord"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/input.rs",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/xdg_shell.rs",
        path "crates/lege-window/**",
        path "shell/lege-shell/src/surface/field_screen.rs",
        path "shell/lege-shell/src/wayland_bridge.rs"
    ]
    intent """
        Give the user a way to close and resize a window, and give every declared action a mouse route. `window.close` is declared in the action manifest and reachable from nothing: lege_shell_toplevel_v1.close exists in the protocol and ShellBridge::close exists in the shell with zero callers, so a window with no client-side decorations — most XWayland clients, xterm, SDL applications — cannot be closed by the user at all. Interactive resize is a deliberate no-op in xdg_shell.rs::resize_request, and decoration mode is forced to ClientSide unconditionally, so such a window can never be resized either. Separately, thirteen declared actions are reachable only by keyboard; the action manifest's own rule is that a chord is an accelerator recorded alongside a menu entry and never the only route to a capability, so those need a pointer or menu path.
        """
    note """
        STATUS FOR A HANDOFF: implemented, tested and lint-clean; see @legeos.evidence.window-controls-20260825. All four steps landed, including interactive resize. check-actions now enforces the pointer-route rule: a declared action with a chord and no pointer route in shell/lege-shell/src (needles: FieldAction::RunAction, WidgetRegion::new, MenuItem::new; test modules excluded) fails the build; exemptions are (id, reason) pairs that self-expire when stale, and the list is empty. window.close goes through a new secondary-click context menu with a two-step confirm per its confirm:true. Interactive resize works on both routes: xdg_toplevel.resize / X11 _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE for clients with frames, and a painted compositor-owned bottom-right grip for no-CSD windows; minimum window size is derived so a minimum-size window still has both a grabbable band and a grip. Decoration forcing is untouched — no server-side decorations were built; band+grip is what makes a no-CSD window manageable. A latent grab bug was fixed along the way: band grabs are now primary-button-only (a right-press previously installed a grab only a left release could end).

        The one outstanding check is hands-on at a real desktop, same as the foundations record; complete this after that run.

        FOR A SUCCESSOR: (1) window.maximize is not in the manifest and ShellBridge::set_maximized is still uncalled — closing that is one declaration, one MenuScope::Window row and one perform_on_window arm. (2) The close confirm UI lives in the shell by design, so a no-CSD window cannot be closed while the shell is dead — only moved, resized and escaped. (3) begin_resize refuses maximized windows (their geometry is re-derived on every topology change); unmaximize first. (4) The shell's cycle order is window_id, the compositor's is stacking order — same set, different order, only one route in use at a time. (5) Menu cycle/group entries are computed shell-side reusing lege_window::geometry::contiguous_window, so the group rule has one implementation.
        """
    part_of [ @legeos.work.home-widgets ]
    verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.window-controls-20260825 ]
}

